 ### [Scanline A-buffer rendering](12488-scanline-a-buffer-rendering.md)
-A rendering algorithm for surface-defined objects in hybrid scenes. It creates an a-buffer for a scanline, containing a list for each pixel of all fragments that partially or fully cover the pixel. It sets itself apart by explicitly managing fragment lists per pixel to support hybrid volume rendering, unlike standard scanline renderers that discard depth information early.
+A rendering algorithm for surface-defined objects in hybrid scenes that creates an a-buffer for a scanline containing a list for each pixel of all fragments that partially or fully cover the pixel. It sets itself apart by explicitly managing fragment lists per pixel to support hybrid volume rendering, unlike standard scanline renderers that discard depth information early.
 
 ### Giertsen's scan-plane buffer algorithm
-A hybrid volume rendering method that traverses the volume in object order and accumulates contributions in image order (object-then-image traversal), using a 2D scan-plane buffer to store information perpendicular to a scanline [S2]. It discretizes the z-dimension due to the scan-plane buffer. The algorithm processes scanlines from top to bottom and determines the intersections of the cells with the respective scan-plane in an incremental manner using a list of active cells whose y-extents cover the current scanline [S2]. The volume elements intersecting the current scan-plane are sliced by finding the edge intersections of faces of volume elements with the current scan-plane [S2]. Then, each slice is divided into triangles and each triangle is further decomposed into line segments in the z direction [S2]. The composition is carried out by processing the line segments in front-to-back order and linearly interpolating them along the ray [S2]. The quality of the images and the performance of the algorithm are heavily dependent on the discretization level of the scan-plane buffer [S2].
